## Constraints

- In Comparison Mode: show **2-4 candidates max** in the table.
- In Auto-Pilot Mode: pick exactly **1 skill** and execute immediately.
- In Hybrid Mode: ask exactly **1 open-ended question**, then route.
- Always verify skill existence before recommending (✅ installed / ⚠️ unverified).
- Prefer **specific skills** over generic ones when the stack is known.
- Auto-detect the stack from project files — do NOT ask if detectable.
- Always include "Why not the others" section in Comparison Mode.
- After selection, **read SKILL.md and begin work** — never stop at recommendation.
- If no skill fits, offer the 3-option smart fallback (manual / create new / use generic).
- Respond in the **same language** the user used.
- In Auto-Pilot, **always pause for dangerous operations** (Safety Gate).
- Track session decisions to avoid repetition (Session Memory).

---

## Failure Shields

- Do NOT recommend skills that are not verified as installed.
- Do NOT ask more than 1 question when project context is available.
- Do NOT present a comparison table with only 1 option — just recommend directly.
- Do NOT ignore project files (AGENTS.md, package.json, open files) in favor of questions.
- Do NOT recommend the same skill under multiple names in the table.
- Do NOT execute destructive operations in Auto-Pilot without confirmation.
- Do NOT re-recommend a skill the user already rejected in this session.
- Do NOT switch languages mid-response — maintain consistency.
- Do NOT skip the "Why not others" section in Comparison Mode.
- Do NOT present a pipeline of more than 4 skills — simplify if longer.

---

## Limitations

- Only recommends skills from the installed library.
- Routing is heuristic. Truly ambiguous goals may need 1 follow-up question.
- The routing reference covers common patterns but not every installed skill.
- In Auto-Pilot, the agent's choice may not always match the user's preference — override is always available.
- Session memory is conversation-scoped only; it does not persist across conversations.
- Estimated times are rough approximations and depend on task complexity.
